        "titulo": "The four forms of \"who\", step by step",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: \"who?\" changes with the CASE, like the pronouns — English still has who / whom / whose: «wer» (nominative, subject): «Wer kommt?» (Who's coming?); «wen» (accusative, direct object): «Wen siehst du?» (Whom do you see?); «wem» (dative): «Wem hilfst du?» (Who are you helping? — to whom); «wessen» (genitive): «Wessen Auto ist das?» (Whose car is that?). STEP 2: with a PREPOSITION, the preposition comes first and decides the case — no dangling prepositions as in English \"Who are you talking to?\": «Mit wem sprichst du?» (mit + dative), «Für wen ist das?» (für + accusative), «Von wem ist der Brief?» (von + dative), «Zu wem gehst du?» (zu + dative). STEP 3: the answer uses the same case: «Mit wem sprichst du? — Mit meinem Chef.» STEP 4: remember the prepositions and the cases they take (lessons 28 and 29).",
